Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 11:18:14 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON: Futures vs. Equities (was: Stupidity and natural selection)

Perry wrote:
>After all, futures are a zero sum game, unlike, say, the equities market. 

I asked:
> I think I disagree.  Can you offer a more precise definition of
> "zero-sum" so that we could evaluate your claim?  Both futures and
> equities offer a net return on one's investment, returns competitive
> with each other when corrected for market-correlated risk.  

Perry responded:
>Equities are shares in companies. If the company gets more valuable, it
>is possible for an investor to get richer without anyone else getting
>poorer. On the other hand, the futures market is a betting market in which
>whenever I make money it is because someone else has lost it. 

This is not precise enough for me.  To ground your thinking, consider
the following observations:

1) As I said above, the net average return of futures investors is
positive, not zero.  So they could *all* make money relative to where
they started.

2) If we ignore voting rights and dividends, owning a stock is
equivalent to betting on a company's stock, if what we are betting is
a index "market asset", an average of all stocks weighted by their
current market value.   Your stock can only go up relative to the
index if someone else's goes down.  Does this make stocks zero-sum?

3) What if they offered futures on individual stocks?  Again ignoring
dividends, the current futures price should just equal the current
stock price.  There's no point in offering such futures, because
there's no difference.  How then could one be zero-sum and the other
not?

Robin Hanson

