Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 11:18:26 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: PPL: Capriciousness?

Is privately-produced law (PPL) more capricious than statist law?

(Hard to believe, I know, but let me explore this possibility...)

The recent example of PPL-on-this-list, the case of Singh v. Metzger,
has bothered me. In a nutshell, I felt Harry Shapiro's "punishment" of
Perry was unwarranted, given that we all make sweeping generalizations
at times. (See, that's a sweeping generalization, unsupported by
footnotes, by opinion polls, and other forms of proof. Should someone
say: "I charge Mr. May with making an unsupported statement,"
presumably under the Singh v. Metzger precedent, Harry would
investigate the claim, conclude that I had indeed made an unsupported
statement, and then decide on some form of punishment. This is what
I'm getting at here!)

The "capriciousness" aspect comes from the unpredictability of
enforcement. Under statist law (not advocated by me...), what laws
there are are presumably enforced on all. (We know this isn't always
the case, of course.)

Perry Metzger gets punished because Pandit Singh lodges a formal
complaint about an opinion/generalization Perry made. Meanwhile,
similar sweeping generalizations are made *hourly* by other list
members, myself included, and because nobody files a complaint, we go
unpunished. For example, Mr. Singh has recently made more assertions
about yogi levitation, unsupported by documentation of any kind other
than citing mythology. Should he be charged? 

And much more seriously than anything Perry ever said, he recently
claimed that Yuri Geller has successfully sued James Randi, an
assertion he didn't back up (and which is false). (Caveat: I can't
confirm the exact phrasing from within my mail editor...)

I don't favor charging Mr. Singh, but I consider it ironic that _his_
broad generalizations are left "unpunished" while he charges Perry
with making unfounded statements!  (and it seems that Perry's comments
were in private e-mail, but that's another story).

Thus, our current form of PPL on this list seems to favor
"complainers" and the "thin-skinned." A parallel, perhaps, to the
clogging of our courts with frivolous cases for which others pick up
the tab (a situation examined carefully by Benson in "The Enterprise
of Law"). (My case would be stronger if in fact there had been _many_
cases similar to the Singh v. Metzger case. Oh well.)

Some possible fixes:

* a fee for bringing charges. This stops the subsidizing of "court
costs" by the larger community (well, Harry's time) and may put a
damper on future "abuses."

* a recognition that what _many_ members of the community do (e.g.,
make sweeping generalizations on this list) to some extent establishes
what is and what is not a violation of the customary laws. (In the
case of Singh v. Metzger, Perry's "violation" seemed to many of us to
be no violation at all, despite Harry's interpretation of the formal
list policies...the ruling seemed to me to be overly legalistic, which
is the whole area I'm addressing here. Needless to say, again, I am
not criticizing Harry. In fact, I think examples like this are
immensely helpful in explicating the nature of PPL in communities
like ours.)

* reputations. Those who file charges a lot, on fairly frivolous or
thin-skinned bases, can have their reputations suffer. Much the same
way little children who say "I'm _telling_!" see their reputations
tarnished. 

* Token punishment. The court recognizes the formal truth of the
charges (e.g., that Perry failed to provide footnotes for his
generalization) but also recognizes the true damage done (e.g., Pandit
was not really damaged by Perry's generalization). The penalty or
punishment is thus minimal (as in the "One dollar" fines we see in
some court cases.)

(A sidenote: If _restitution_ is the goal of PPL, and not
_punishment_, then why is our list set up the way it is? Might it not
be useful to look for ways of simulating restitution? Just a thought.)

I have hope that customary law can better handle such situations than
statist law can, but I'm slightly concerned that our present system
seems to be capricious.


